--TEST--
`$fn(...)` calls a closure through the variable holding it, and reaches the same body a `Core` member's callback does — `rule:types/closure-literal` and `rule:types/implicit-capture`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:expressions/closures-fn
// `rule:types/closure-literal`: `callable` is the only closure type and it carries no
// parameter list, so a call through one is typed `mixed` and converts on
// request like any other `mixed` — there is nothing narrower to type it.
callable $upper = fn (string $s): string => Core\Str::upper($s);

// Agreement, not one answer: the same closure called directly and handed to
// `Core\Arr::map`, which reaches the closure's `invoke` through
// `nvs_runtime::call_closure` too. A direct call that grew a second entry
// point with a convention of its own would fail here while each half still
// printed plausibly on its own line. A bare `callable` promises no signature
// and `map`'s callback asks for one (`rule:types/callable-signature`), so it
// travels there inside a literal that forwards to it.
array<string> $words = ["ab", "cd", "ef"];
array<mixed> $mapped = Core\Arr::map($words, fn (string $w) => $upper($w));
int $agree = 0;
int $i = 0;
foreach ($words as string $w) {
    string $direct = $upper($w) as string;
    if ($direct == ($mapped[$i as string] as string)) {
        $agree = $agree + 1;
    }
    $i = $i + 1;
}
echo "agree ", $agree as string, " of ", Core\Arr::count($words) as string, "\n";

// `rule:types/implicit-capture`: capture is implicit, by value, and snapshotted where the
// literal is evaluated — so rebinding the captured local afterwards is not
// visible to the closure. There is no `use (&$prefix)` to make it so.
string $prefix = "one";
callable $tag = fn (string $s): string => $prefix . ":" . $s;
$prefix = "two";
echo $tag("x"), "\n";

// Both sides of the arity bound. A closure may declare *fewer* parameters
// than the call passes — spec § 1's callback rule, which is also PHP's answer
// for extra positional arguments — and the extras are dropped rather than
// being an error.
callable $first = fn (int $a): int => $a;
echo $first(7, 8, 9) as string, "\n";
callable $seven = fn (): int => 7;
echo $seven() as string, "\n";

// The other side: fewer arguments than the closure declares is a catchable
// throw, not an engine fault. Nothing checked it earlier — a `callable` has
// no parameter list for the checker to count against — so `rule:errors/propagation`'s checked
// return is where it lands.
callable $add = fn (int $a, int $b): int => $a + $b;
try {
    echo $add(1) as string, "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "caught: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// The callee's own throw travels back unchanged rather than being replaced by
// a message from the call site.
callable $half = fn (int $n): float => $n / 0;
try {
    echo $half(1) as string, "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "caught: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// A closure literal called where it is written, and one read back out of an
// array: the callee is an expression, not a name a call site resolved.
echo (fn (int $n): int => $n + 1)(41) as string, "\n";
array<callable> $wrappers = [
    fn (string $s): string => "[" . $s . "]",
    fn (string $s): string => "<" . $s . ">",
];
foreach ($wrappers as callable $wrap) {
    echo $wrap("v"), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
agree 3 of 3
one:x
7
7
caught: too few arguments to a `callable`: it declares 2 parameter(s), 1 given
caught: Division by zero
42
[v]
<v>
